  "body": "﻿## What changed\n\nSibling of #3873 (the server side, PR pending). On Windows a crashed window leaves its discovery file `<pid>.json`, and the CLI opened a session with any peer on that stale port that answered `HELLO_OK` - the peer never proved that it knew the token. This PR closes the client side, all three closes from the issue:\n\n- **The token proof.** The CLI sends a fresh nonce in its hello (`camcraft_link`/`token`/`nonce`). The window answers `HELLO_OK` extended with a `proof`: two FNV-1a 64-bit words (the published public-domain parameters) over `token`, a zero byte, `nonce`. No new crypto dependency - the token itself already crosses the same socket in the clear, so the proof only binds the answer to a peer that read the discovery file and saw this client's fresh nonce. The client refuses a peer whose answer does not prove it. A hello without a nonce (an older CLI) still gets the plain `HELLO_OK` answer, so old CLIs keep working against a new window.\n- **The pid-alive check.** Before it connects, the CLI checks that the pid in each entry is alive (`kill(pid, 0)` on Unix, `OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION)` + `GetExitCodeProcess` on Windows) and removes the file of a dead pid - never a live window's file. When the check cannot say (an access error), the entry stays and the token proof guards the connection. `libc` (unix) and `windows-sys` (Windows) joined as target deps; both are already in the lock through other deps - no new crate.\n- **The size and deadline limits.** One answer line is bounded at 1 MiB (`MAX_ANSWER`), a `--ui mcp` bridge session at 256 MiB (`MAX_BRIDGE`); the `HELLO_TIMEOUT` deadline (5 s) covers the whole proof exchange. After the proof, no read deadline applies on purpose: a tool may run for minutes (#3751), and the peer has then proved that it read the discovery file, so the bound on what it may send is the remaining check.\n\n## The pin\n\n`crates/app/tests/mcp_live/ui_link.rs`:\n\n- `a_false_window_that_does_not_prove_the_token_gets_no_tool_call`: a false server on the port of a discovery entry (it has the token, a live pid) answers a plain `HELLO_OK` with no proof. The CLI exits 1, and the only line the false server received is the hello with the nonce - no `tools/call`.\n- `a_cli_call_with_no_window_says_so_and_exits_3` now also pins that the CLI removes the file of a dead pid, and does not remove a live pid's file.\n\n## Gate\n\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app --test mcp_live ui_link`: 7 passed.\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app --lib`: 307 passed.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No test drives `--ui mcp` (`bridge`) against a peer that overruns `MAX_BRIDGE`; the bound is the same take-based read as the call path.\n- The refusal of a recorded (replayed) proof answer is covered by the nonce design, not by a test.\n- Only expected overlap with #3873: both-added tests in `crates/app/tests/mcp_live/ui_link.rs`.\n\nChangelog: the CLI UI link now talks only to a window that proves the link token, and removes the stale link file of a dead pid (Windows local-user hardening, #3874)\n\nFixes #3874\r\n",
